Phase 1: Create Infrastructure

✅ Launch an AWS EC2 Ubuntu instance.
Phase 2: Prepare the Server

Connect to EC2 using SSH:

ssh -i key.pem ubuntu@<public-ip>

Then update the server:

sudo apt update
sudo apt upgrade -y
Phase 3: Install Required Software

You installed:

✅ Java 17 (required for Jenkins)

java -version

✅ Docker

docker --version        
sudo systemctl start docker
sudo systemctl enable docker
sudo systemctl status docker



Phase 4: Run Jenkins using Docker


Step 1: Create the Docker volume
sudo docker volume create jenkins_home
Step 2: Pull the Jenkins image
sudo docker pull jenkins/jenkins:lts-jdk17
Step 3: Run Jenkins

Now execute this exact command:

sudo docker run -d \
  --name jenkins \
  -p 8080:8080 \
  -p 50000:50000 \
  -v jenkins_home:/var/jenkins_home \
  jenkins/jenkins:lts-jdk17
Run Jenkins:

Step 4: Verify
Check the container:

docker ps
Step 5: Get the Jenkins password
Get the admin password:

docker exec jenkins cat /var/jenkins_home/secrets/initialAdminPassword
Step 6: Open Jenkins

http://EC2-PUBLIC-IP:8080


Phase 5: Configure Jenkins

Inside Jenkins:
Step 1: Enter the Jenkins container (from the EC2 terminal)
sudo docker exec -u root -it jenkins bash

✅ Install tools

Git
Maven
JDK

(You installed Maven inside the Jenkins container for practice.
In production, many teams use Jenkins tool configuration or prebuilt Docker images
Step 2: Update package information
apt update
Step 3: (Optional for learning)
apt upgrade -y

In production, people usually avoid apt upgrade inside containers because containers are meant to be immutable. For learning, it's okay.

